      <h1 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting the installation<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Troubleshoot the OpenShift Container Platform installation process to identify and resolve deployment failures.</p>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-install-workflow_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting the installation program workflow<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-install-workflow_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Before troubleshooting the installation environment, it is critical to understand the overall flow of the installer-provisioned installation on bare metal. </p>
      <p>The following diagrams illustrate a troubleshooting flow with a step-by-step breakdown for the environment.</p>
      <p><strong>Figure 1. Troubleshooting workflow for </strong><code>install-config.yaml</code><strong> file errors or inaccessible RHCOS images</strong></p>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/flow1.png" alt="Troubleshooting workflow when the `install-config.yaml` file has errors or the Red&amp;#160;Hat Enterprise Linux CoreOS (RHCOS) images are inaccessible" />
      <p>This diagram illustrates a troubleshooting workflow when the <code>install-config.yaml</code> file has errors or the Red Hat Enterprise Linux CoreOS (RHCOS) images are inaccessible. See  <a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-install-config_ipi-install-troubleshooting">Troubleshooting <code>install-config.yaml</code></a> for troubleshooting suggestions.</p>
      <p><strong>Figure 2. Troubleshooting workflow for bootstrap VM issues, bootstrap VMs that cannot boot up the cluster nodes, and inspecting logs</strong></p>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/flow2.png" alt="Troubleshooting workflow for bootstrap VM issues" />
      <p>This diagram illustrates a troubleshooting workflow for <a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm_ipi-install-troubleshooting"> bootstrap VM issues</a>, <a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm-cannot-boot_ipi-install-troubleshooting"> bootstrap VMs that cannot boot up the cluster nodes</a>, and  <a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm-inspecting-logs_ipi-install-troubleshooting"> inspecting logs</a>. When installing an OpenShift Container Platform cluster without the <code>provisioning</code> network, this workflow does not apply.</p>
      <p><strong>Figure 3. Troubleshooting workflow for cluster nodes that will not PXE boot</strong></p>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/flow3.png" alt="Troubleshooting workflow for cluster nodes that will not PXE boot" />
      <p>This diagram illustrates a troubleshooting workflow for <a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-cluster-nodes-will-not-pxe_ipi-install-troubleshooting">cluster nodes that will not PXE boot</a>. If installing using Redfish virtual media, each node must meet minimum firmware requirements for the installation program to deploy the node. For additional details, see <a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/installing/installing_bare_metal/ipi/ipi-install-prerequisites#ipi-install-firmware-requirements-for-installing-with-virtual-media_ipi-install-prerequisites">Firmware requirements for installing with virtual media</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>Figure 4. Troubleshooting workflow from a non-accessible API to a validated installation</strong></p>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/flow4.png" alt="Troubleshooting workflow from a non-accessible API to a validated installation" />
      <p>This diagram illustrates a troubleshooting workflow from <a href="#investigating-an-unavailable-kubernetes-api_ipi-install-troubleshooting"> a non-accessible API</a> to a <a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-reviewing-the-installation_ipi-install-troubleshooting">validated installation</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-install-config_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting <code>install-config.yaml</code><a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-install-config_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The <code>install-config.yaml</code> configuration file represents all of the nodes that are part of the OpenShift Container Platform cluster. The file contains the necessary options consisting of but not limited to <code>apiVersion</code>, <code>baseDomain</code>, <code>imageContentSources</code> and virtual IP addresses. </p>
      <p>If errors occur early in the deployment of the OpenShift Container Platform cluster, the errors are likely in the <code>install-config.yaml</code> configuration file.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Use the guidelines in <a href="https://www.redhat.com/sysadmin/yaml-tips">YAML-tips</a>.</li>
      <li>Verify the YAML syntax is correct using <a href="http://www.yamllint.com/">syntax-check</a>.</li>
      <li>
      Verify the Red Hat Enterprise Linux CoreOS (RHCOS) QEMU images are properly defined and accessible via the URL provided in the <code>install-config.yaml</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ curl -s -o /dev/null -I -w \"%{http_code}\\n\" http://webserver.example.com:8080/rhcos-44.81.202004250133-0-qemu.<architecture>.qcow2.gz?sha256=7d884b46ee54fe87bbc3893bf2aa99af3b2d31f2e19ab5529c60636fbd0f1ce7"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If the output is <code>200</code>, there is a valid response from the webserver storing the bootstrap VM image.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting bootstrap VM issues<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The OpenShift Container Platform installation program spawns a bootstrap node virtual machine, which handles provisioning the OpenShift Container Platform cluster nodes.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      About 10 to 15 minutes after triggering the installation program, ensure the bootstrap VM is operational by running the <code>virsh</code> command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo virsh list"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={" Id    Name                           State\n --------------------------------------------\n 12    openshift-xf6fq-bootstrap      running"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The name of the bootstrap VM is always the cluster name followed by a random set of characters and ending in the word "bootstrap."</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      If the bootstrap VM is not running after 10-15 minutes, verify <code>libvirtd</code> is running on the system by using the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ systemctl status libvirtd"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"● libvirtd.service - Virtualization daemon\n   Loaded: loaded (/usr/lib/systemd/system/libvirtd.service; enabled; vendor preset: enabled)\n   Active: active (running) since Tue 2020-03-03 21:21:07 UTC; 3 weeks 5 days ago\n     Docs: man:libvirtd(8)\n           https://libvirt.org\n Main PID: 9850 (libvirtd)\n    Tasks: 20 (limit: 32768)\n   Memory: 74.8M\n   CGroup: /system.slice/libvirtd.service\n           ├─ 9850 /usr/sbin/libvirtd"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>If the bootstrap VM is operational, log in to it.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Find the IP address of the bootstrap VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo virsh console example.com"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"Connected to domain example.com\nEscape character is ^]\nRed Hat Enterprise Linux CoreOS 43.81.202001142154.0 (Ootpa) 4.3\nSSH host key: SHA256:BRWJktXZgQQRY5zjuAV0IKZ4WM7i4TiUyMVanqu9Pqg (ED25519)\nSSH host key: SHA256:7+iKGA7VtG5szmk2jB5gl/5EZ+SNcJ3a2g23o0lnIio (ECDSA)\nSSH host key: SHA256:DH5VWhvhvagOTaLsYiVNse9ca+ZSW/30OOMed8rIGOc (RSA)\nens3:  fd35:919d:4042:2:c7ed:9a9f:a9ec:7\nens4: 172.22.0.2 fe80::1d05:e52e:be5d:263f\nlocalhost login:"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>When deploying an OpenShift Container Platform cluster without the <code>provisioning</code> network, you must use a public IP address and not a private IP address like <code>172.22.0.2</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      After you obtain the IP address, log in to the bootstrap VM by running the <code>ssh</code> command:
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>In the console output of the previous step, you can use the IPv6 IP address provided by <code>ens3</code> or the IPv4 IP provided by <code>ens4</code>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh core@172.22.0.2"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If you are not successful logging in to the bootstrap VM, you have likely encountered one of the following scenarios:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>You cannot reach the <code>172.22.0.0/24</code> network. Verify the network connectivity between the provisioner and the <code>provisioning</code> network bridge. This issue might occur if you are using a <code>provisioning</code> network.</li>
      <li>You cannot reach the bootstrap VM through the public network. When attempting to SSH via <code>baremetal</code> network, verify connectivity on the <code>provisioner</code> host specifically around the <code>baremetal</code> network bridge.</li>
      <li>You encountered <code>Permission denied (publickey,password,keyboard-interactive)</code>. When attempting to access the bootstrap VM, a <code>Permission denied</code> error might occur. Verify that the SSH key for the user attempting to log in to the VM is set within the <code>install-config.yaml</code> file.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm-cannot-boot_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Bootstrap VM cannot boot up the cluster nodes<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm-cannot-boot_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">During the deployment, it is possible for the bootstrap VM to fail to boot the cluster nodes, which prevents the VM from provisioning the nodes with the RHCOS image. </p>
      <p>This scenario can arise due to:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>A problem with the <code>install-config.yaml</code> file.</li>
      <li>Issues with out-of-band network access when using the baremetal network.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>To verify the issue, there are three containers related to <code>ironic</code>:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><code>ironic</code></li>
      <li><code>ironic-inspector</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Log in to the bootstrap VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh core@172.22.0.2"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the container logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@localhost ~]$ sudo podman logs -f <container_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;container_name&gt;</code> with one of <code>ironic</code> or <code>ironic-inspector</code>. If you encounter an issue where the control plane nodes are not booting up from PXE, check the <code>ironic</code> pod. The <code>ironic</code> pod contains information about the attempt to boot the cluster nodes, because it attempts to log in to the node over IPMI.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Results</strong></p>
      <p>The cluster nodes might be in the <code>ON</code> state when deployment started.</p>
      <p><strong>Troubleshooting</strong></p>
      <p>Power off the OpenShift Container Platform cluster nodes before you begin the installation over IPMI by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ipmitool -I lanplus -U root -P <password> -H <out_of_band_ip> power off"} lang="terminal" />
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm-inspecting-logs_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Inspecting logs<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-bootstrap-vm-inspecting-logs_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">When experiencing issues downloading or accessing the RHCOS images, first verify that the URL is correct in the <code>install-config.yaml</code> configuration file.</p>
      <p>See the following example of internal webserver hosting RHCOS images:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"bootstrapOSImage: http://<ip:port>/rhcos-43.81.202001142154.0-qemu.<architecture>.qcow2.gz?sha256=9d999f55ff1d44f7ed7c106508e5deecd04dc3c06095d34d36bf1cd127837e0c\nclusterOSImage: http://<ip:port>/rhcos-43.81.202001142154.0-openstack.<architecture>.qcow2.gz?sha256=a1bda656fa0892f7b936fdc6b6a6086bddaed5dafacedcd7a1e811abb78fe3b0"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>The <code>coreos-downloader</code> container downloads resources from a webserver or from the external <a href="https://quay.io">quay.io</a> registry, whichever the <code>install-config.yaml</code> configuration file specifies. Verify that the <code>coreos-downloader</code> container is up and running and inspect its logs as needed.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Log in to the bootstrap VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh core@172.22.0.2"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the status of the <code>coreos-downloader</code> container within the bootstrap VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@localhost ~]$ sudo podman logs -f coreos-downloader"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If the bootstrap VM cannot access the URL to the images, use the <code>curl</code> command to verify that the VM can access the images.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Inspect the system and bootstrap logs to verify if all the containers launched during the deployment phase:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Inspect the system logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@localhost ~]$ journalctl -xe"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Inspect the <code>bootkube</code> logs to check bootstrap progress by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@localhost ~]$ journalctl -b -f -u bootkube.service"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify all the pods, including <code>dnsmasq</code>, <code>mariadb</code>, <code>httpd</code>, and <code>ironic</code>, are running by using the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@localhost ~]$ sudo podman ps"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      If there are issues with the pods, check the logs of the containers with issues. To check the logs of the <code>ironic</code> service, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@localhost ~]$ sudo podman logs ironic"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="investigating-an-unavailable-kubernetes-api_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Investigating an unavailable Kubernetes API<a href="#investigating-an-unavailable-kubernetes-api_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">When the Kubernetes API is unavailable, check the control plane nodes to ensure that they are running the correct components. Also, check the hostname resolution.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Ensure that <code>etcd</code> is running on each of the control plane nodes by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo crictl logs $(sudo crictl ps --pod=$(sudo crictl pods --name=etcd-member --quiet) --quiet)"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      If the previous command fails, ensure that Kubelet created the <code>etcd</code> pods by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo crictl pods --name=etcd-member"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If there are no pods, investigate <code>etcd</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the cluster nodes to ensure they have a fully qualified domain name, and not just <code>localhost.localdomain</code>, by using the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ hostname"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If a hostname is not set, set the correct hostname by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo hostnamectl set-hostname <hostname>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Ensure that each node has the correct name resolution in the DNS server by running the <code>dig</code> command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ dig api.<cluster_name>.example.com"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"; <<>> DiG 9.11.4-P2-RedHat-9.11.4-26.P2.el8 <<>> api.<cluster_name>.example.com\n;; global options: +cmd\n;; Got answer:\n;; ->>HEADER<<- opcode: QUERY, status: NOERROR, id: 37551\n;; flags: qr aa rd ra; QUERY: 1, ANSWER: 1, AUTHORITY: 1, ADDITIONAL: 2\n\n;; OPT PSEUDOSECTION:\n; EDNS: version: 0, flags:; udp: 4096\n; COOKIE: 866929d2f8e8563582af23f05ec44203d313e50948d43f60 (good)\n;; QUESTION SECTION:\n;api.<cluster_name>.example.com. IN A\n\n;; ANSWER SECTION:\napi.<cluster_name>.example.com. 10800 IN\tA 10.19.13.86\n\n;; AUTHORITY SECTION:\n<cluster_name>.example.com. 10800 IN NS\t<cluster_name>.example.com.\n\n;; ADDITIONAL SECTION:\n<cluster_name>.example.com. 10800 IN A\t10.19.14.247\n\n;; Query time: 0 msec\n;; SERVER: 10.19.14.247#53(10.19.14.247)\n;; WHEN: Tue May 19 20:30:59 UTC 2020\n;; MSG SIZE  rcvd: 140"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>The output in the foregoing example indicates that the appropriate IP address for the <code>api.&lt;cluster_name&gt;.example.com</code> VIP is <code>10.19.13.86</code>. This IP address should reside on the <code>baremetal</code> network.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="troubleshooting-failure-to-initialize-the-cluster_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting a failure to initialize the cluster<a href="#troubleshooting-failure-to-initialize-the-cluster_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The installation program uses the Cluster Version Operator to create all the components of an OpenShift Container Platform cluster. When the installation program fails to initialize the cluster, you can retrieve the most important information from the <code>ClusterVersion</code> and <code>ClusterOperator</code> objects.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Inspect the <code>ClusterVersion</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusterversion -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\nkind: ClusterVersion\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: 2019-02-27T22:24:21Z\n  generation: 1\n  name: version\n  resourceVersion: \"19927\"\n  selfLink: /apis/config.openshift.io/v1/clusterversions/version\n  uid: 6e0f4cf8-3ade-11e9-9034-0a923b47ded4\nspec:\n  channel: stable-4.1\n  clusterID: 5ec312f9-f729-429d-a454-61d4906896ca\nstatus:\n  availableUpdates: null\n  conditions:\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:50:30Z\n    message: Done applying 4.1.1\n    status: \"True\"\n    type: Available\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:50:30Z\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: Failing\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:50:30Z\n    message: Cluster version is 4.1.1\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: Progressing\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:24:31Z\n    message: 'Unable to retrieve available updates: unknown version 4.1.1\n    reason: RemoteFailed\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: RetrievedUpdates\n  desired:\n    image: registry.svc.ci.openshift.org/openshift/origin-release@sha256:91e6f754975963e7db1a9958075eb609ad226968623939d262d1cf45e9dbc39a\n    version: 4.1.1\n  history:\n  - completionTime: 2019-02-27T22:50:30Z\n    image: registry.svc.ci.openshift.org/openshift/origin-release@sha256:91e6f754975963e7db1a9958075eb609ad226968623939d262d1cf45e9dbc39a\n    startedTime: 2019-02-27T22:24:31Z\n    state: Completed\n    version: 4.1.1\n  observedGeneration: 1\n  versionHash: Wa7as_ik1qE="} lang="yaml" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      View the conditions by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusterversion version \\\n     -o=jsonpath='{range .status.conditions[*]}{.type}{\" \"}{.status}{\" \"}{.message}{\"\\n\"}{end}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Some of most important conditions include <code>Failing</code>, <code>Available</code> and <code>Progressing</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Available True Done applying 4.1.1\nFailing False\nProgressing False Cluster version is 4.0.0-0.alpha-2019-02-26-194020\nRetrievedUpdates False Unable to retrieve available updates: unknown version 4.1.1"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Inspect the <code>ClusterOperator</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusteroperator"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The command returns the status of the cluster Operators.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                  VERSION   AVAILABLE   PROGRESSING   FAILING   SINCE\ncluster-baremetal-operator                      True        False         False     17m\ncluster-autoscaler                              True        False         False     17m\ncluster-storage-operator                        True        False         False     10m\nconsole                                         True        False         False     7m21s\ndns                                             True        False         False     31m\nimage-registry                                  True        False         False     9m58s\ningress                                         True        False         False     10m\nkube-apiserver                                  True        False         False     28m\nkube-controller-manager                         True        False         False     21m\nkube-scheduler                                  True        False         False     25m\nmachine-api                                     True        False         False     17m\nmachine-config                                  True        False         False     17m\nmarketplace-operator                            True        False         False     10m\nmonitoring                                      True        False         False     8m23s\nnetwork                                         True        False         False     13m\nnode-tuning                                     True        False         False     11m\nopenshift-apiserver                             True        False         False     15m\nopenshift-authentication                        True        False         False     20m\nopenshift-cloud-credential-operator             True        False         False     18m\nopenshift-controller-manager                    True        False         False     10m\nopenshift-samples                               True        False         False     8m42s\noperator-lifecycle-manager                      True        False         False     17m\nservice-ca                                      True        False         False     30m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Inspect individual cluster Operators by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusteroperator <operator> -oyaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;operator&gt;</code> with the name of a cluster Operator. This command is useful for identifying why a cluster Operator has not achieved the <code>Available</code> state or is in the <code>Failed</code> state.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\nkind: ClusterOperator\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: 2019-02-27T22:47:04Z\n  generation: 1\n  name: monitoring\n  resourceVersion: \"24677\"\n  selfLink: /apis/config.openshift.io/v1/clusteroperators/monitoring\n  uid: 9a6a5ef9-3ae1-11e9-bad4-0a97b6ba9358\nspec: {}\nstatus:\n  conditions:\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:49:10Z\n    message: Successfully rolled out the stack.\n    status: \"True\"\n    type: Available\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:49:10Z\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: Progressing\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:49:10Z\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: Failing\n  extension: null\n  relatedObjects: null\n  version: \"\""} lang="yaml" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Get the cluster Operator’s status condition by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusteroperator <operator> \\\n     -o=jsonpath='{range .status.conditions[*]}{.type}{\" \"}{.status}{\" \"}{.message}{\"\\n\"}{end}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;operator&gt;</code> with the name of one of the operators above.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Available True Successfully rolled out the stack\nProgressing False\nFailing False"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Retrieve the list of objects owned by the cluster Operator by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusteroperator kube-apiserver \\\n   -o=jsonpath='{.status.relatedObjects}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"[map[resource:kubeapiservers group:operator.openshift.io name:cluster] map[group: name:openshift-config resource:namespaces] map[group: name:openshift-config-managed resource:namespaces] map[group: name:openshift-kube-apiserver-operator resource:namespaces] map[group: name:openshift-kube-apiserver resource:namespaces]]"} lang="javascript" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="troubleshooting-failure-to-fetch-the-console-url_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting a failure to fetch the console URL<a href="#troubleshooting-failure-to-fetch-the-console-url_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The installation program retrieves the URL for the OpenShift Container Platform console by using <code>[route][route-object]</code> within the <code>openshift-console</code> namespace. </p>
      <p>If the installation program fails the retrieve the URL for the console, use the following procedure.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check if the console router is in the <code>Available</code> or <code>Failing</code> state by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get clusteroperator console -oyaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\nkind: ClusterOperator\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: 2019-02-27T22:46:57Z\n  generation: 1\n  name: console\n  resourceVersion: \"19682\"\n  selfLink: /apis/config.openshift.io/v1/clusteroperators/console\n  uid: 960364aa-3ae1-11e9-bad4-0a97b6ba9358\nspec: {}\nstatus:\n  conditions:\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:46:58Z\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: Failing\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:50:12Z\n    status: \"False\"\n    type: Progressing\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:50:12Z\n    status: \"True\"\n    type: Available\n  - lastTransitionTime: 2019-02-27T22:46:57Z\n    status: \"True\"\n    type: Upgradeable\n  extension: null\n  relatedObjects:\n  - group: operator.openshift.io\n    name: cluster\n    resource: consoles\n  - group: config.openshift.io\n    name: cluster\n    resource: consoles\n  - group: oauth.openshift.io\n    name: console\n    resource: oauthclients\n  - group: \"\"\n    name: openshift-console-operator\n    resource: namespaces\n  - group: \"\"\n    name: openshift-console\n    resource: namespaces\n  versions: null"} lang="yaml" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Manually retrieve the console URL by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get route console -n openshift-console \\\n     -o=jsonpath='{.spec.host}' console-openshift-console.apps.adahiya-1.devcluster.openshift.com"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="troubleshooting-failure-to-add-the-ingress-certificate-to-kubeconfig_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting a failure to add the ingress certificate to kubeconfig<a href="#troubleshooting-failure-to-add-the-ingress-certificate-to-kubeconfig_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The installation program adds the default ingress certificate to the list of trusted client certificate authorities in <code>${"{"}{"{"} INSTALL_DIR {"}"}{"}"}/auth/kubeconfig</code>. If the installation program fails to add the ingress certificate to the <code>kubeconfig</code> file, you can retrieve the certificate from the cluster and add it.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Retrieve the certificate from the cluster by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig get configmaps default-ingress-cert \\\n     -n openshift-config-managed -o=jsonpath='{.data.ca-bundle\\.crt}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\nMIIC/TCCAeWgAwIBAgIBATANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQsFADAuMSwwKgYDVQQDDCNjbHVz\ndGVyLWluZ3Jlc3Mtb3BlcmF0b3JAMTU1MTMwNzU4OTAeFw0xOTAyMjcyMjQ2Mjha\nFw0yMTAyMjYyMjQ2MjlaMC4xLDAqBgNVBAMMI2NsdXN0ZXItaW5ncmVzcy1vcGVy\nYXRvckAxNTUxMzA3NTg5MIIBIjANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAAOCAQ8AMIIBCgKCAQEA\nuCA4fQ+2YXoXSUL4h/mcvJfrgpBfKBW5hfB8NcgXeCYiQPnCKblH1sEQnI3VC5Pk\n2OfNCF3PUlfm4i8CHC95a7nCkRjmJNg1gVrWCvS/ohLgnO0BvszSiRLxIpuo3C4S\nEVqqvxValHcbdAXWgZLQoYZXV7RMz8yZjl5CfhDaaItyBFj3GtIJkXgUwp/5sUfI\nLDXW8MM6AXfuG+kweLdLCMm3g8WLLfLBLvVBKB+4IhIH7ll0buOz04RKhnYN+Ebw\ntcvFi55vwuUCWMnGhWHGEQ8sWm/wLnNlOwsUz7S1/sW8nj87GFHzgkaVM9EOnoNI\ngKhMBK9ItNzjrP6dgiKBCQIDAQABoyYwJDAOBgNVHQ8BAf8EBAMCAqQwEgYDVR0T\nAQH/BAgwBgEB/wIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQsFAAOCAQEAq+vi0sFKudaZ9aUQMMha\nCeWx9CZvZBblnAWT/61UdpZKpFi4eJ2d33lGcfKwHOi2NP/iSKQBebfG0iNLVVPz\nvwLbSG1i9R9GLdAbnHpPT9UG6fLaDIoKpnKiBfGENfxeiq5vTln2bAgivxrVlyiq\n+MdDXFAWb6V4u2xh6RChI7akNsS3oU9PZ9YOs5e8vJp2YAEphht05X0swA+X8V8T\nC278FFifpo0h3Q0Dbv8Rfn4UpBEtN4KkLeS+JeT+0o2XOsFZp7Uhr9yFIodRsnNo\nH/Uwmab28ocNrGNiEVaVH6eTTQeeZuOdoQzUbClElpVmkrNGY0M42K0PvOQ/e7+y\nAQ==\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>Add the certificate to the <code>client-certificate-authority-data</code> field in the <code>${"{"}{"{"} INSTALL_DIR {"}"}{"}"}/auth/kubeconfig</code> file.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="troubleshooting-ssh-access-to-cluster-nodes_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting SSH access to cluster nodes<a href="#troubleshooting-ssh-access-to-cluster-nodes_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">For added security, you cannot SSH into the cluster from outside the cluster by default. However, you can access control plane and worker nodes from the provisioner node. If you cannot SSH into the cluster nodes from the provisioner node, the nodes might be waiting on the bootstrap VM. </p>
      <p>The control plane nodes retrieve their boot configuration from the bootstrap VM, and they cannot boot successfully if they do not retrieve the boot configuration.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>If you have physical access to the nodes, check their console output to verify if they have successfully booted. If the nodes are still retrieving their boot configuration, there might be problems with the bootstrap VM .</li>
      <li>Ensure you have configured the <code>sshKey: '&lt;ssh_pub_key&gt;'</code> setting in the <code>install-config.yaml</code> file, where <code>&lt;ssh_pub_key&gt;</code> is the public key of the <code>kni</code> user on the provisioner node.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-cluster-nodes-will-not-pxe_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Cluster nodes do not PXE boot<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-cluster-nodes-will-not-pxe_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">When OpenShift Container Platform cluster nodes do not PXE boot, run the troubleshooting checks on the cluster nodes that do not PXE boot. </p>
      <p>This procedure does not apply when installing an OpenShift Container Platform cluster without the <code>provisioning</code> network.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Check the network connectivity to the <code>provisioning</code> network.</li>
      <li>Ensure PXE is enabled on the NIC for the <code>provisioning</code> network and PXE is disabled for all other NICs.</li>
      <li>
      Verify that the <code>install-config.yaml</code> configuration file includes the <code>rootDeviceHints</code> parameter and boot MAC address for the NIC connected to the <code>provisioning</code> network.
      <p>For example:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Control plane node settings
      <CodeBlock code={"bootMACAddress: 24:6E:96:1B:96:90 # MAC of bootable provisioning NIC"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Compute node settings
      <CodeBlock code={"bootMACAddress: 24:6E:96:1B:96:90 # MAC of bootable provisioning NIC"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="installing-creates-no-worker-nodes_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing creates no worker nodes<a href="#installing-creates-no-worker-nodes_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The installation program does not provision worker nodes directly. Instead, the Machine API Operator scales nodes up and down on supported platforms. </p>
      <p>If worker nodes are not created after 15 to 20 minutes, depending on the speed of the cluster’s internet connection, investigate the Machine API Operator.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check the Machine API Operator by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig \\\n   --namespace=openshift-machine-api get deployments"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If <code>${"{"}{"{"} INSTALL_DIR {"}"}{"}"}</code> is not set in your environment, replace the value with the name of the installation directory.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                          READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\ncluster-autoscaler-operator   1/1     1            1           86m\ncluster-baremetal-operator    1/1     1            1           86m\nmachine-api-controllers       1/1     1            1           85m\nmachine-api-operator          1/1     1            1           86m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the machine controller logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc --kubeconfig=${INSTALL_DIR}/auth/kubeconfig \\\n     --namespace=openshift-machine-api logs deployments/machine-api-controllers \\\n     --container=machine-controller"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="troubleshooting-the-cluster-network-operator_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting the Cluster Network Operator<a href="#troubleshooting-the-cluster-network-operator_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The Cluster Network Operator is responsible for deploying the networking components. It runs early in the installation process, after the control plane nodes have come up but before the installation program removes the bootstrap control plane. Issues with this Operator might indicate installation program issues.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Ensure the network configuration exists by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get network -o yaml cluster"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If it does not exist, the installation program did not create it. To find the reason, run the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openshift-install create manifests"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Review the manifests to determine why the installation program did not create the network configuration.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Ensure the network is running by entering the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get po -n openshift-network-operator"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="unable-to-discover-new-bare-metal-hosts-using-the-bmc_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Unable to discover new bare-metal hosts using the BMC<a href="#unable-to-discover-new-bare-metal-hosts-using-the-bmc_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">In some cases, the installation program is not be able to discover the new bare-metal hosts and issue an error, because it cannot mount the remote virtual media share.</p>
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"ProvisioningError 51s metal3-baremetal-controller Image provisioning failed: Deploy step deploy.deploy failed with BadRequestError: HTTP POST\nhttps://<bmc_address>/redfish/v1/Managers/iDRAC.Embedded.1/VirtualMedia/CD/Actions/VirtualMedia.InsertMedia\nreturned code 400.\nBase.1.8.GeneralError: A general error has occurred. See ExtendedInfo for more information\nExtended information: [\n  {\n    \"Message\": \"Unable to mount remote share https://<ironic_address>/redfish/boot-<uuid>.iso.\",\n    \"MessageArgs\": [\n      \"https://<ironic_address>/redfish/boot-<uuid>.iso\"\n    ],\n    \"MessageArgs@odata.count\": 1,\n    \"MessageId\": \"IDRAC.2.5.RAC0720\",\n    \"RelatedProperties\": [\n      \"#/Image\"\n    ],\n    \"RelatedProperties@odata.count\": 1,\n    \"Resolution\": \"Retry the operation.\",\n    \"Severity\": \"Informational\"\n  }\n]."} lang="terminal" />
      <p>In this situation, if you are using virtual media with an unknown certificate authority, you can configure your baseboard management controller (BMC) remote file share settings to trust an unknown certificate authority to avoid this error.</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>This resolution was tested on OpenShift Container Platform 4.11 with Dell iDRAC 9 and firmware version 5.10.50.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h2 id="worker-nodes-cannot-join-the-cluster_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Troubleshooting worker nodes that cannot join the cluster<a href="#worker-nodes-cannot-join-the-cluster_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Installer-provisioned clusters deploy with a DNS server that includes a DNS entry for the <code>api-int.&lt;cluster_name&gt;.&lt;base_domain&gt;</code> URL. If the nodes within the cluster use an external or upstream DNS server to resolve the <code>api-int.&lt;cluster_name&gt;.&lt;base_domain&gt;</code> URL and there is no such entry, worker nodes might fail to join the cluster. </p>
      <p>Ensure that all nodes in the cluster can resolve the domain name.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Add a DNS A/AAAA or CNAME record to internally identify the API load balancer. For example, when using dnsmasq, modify the <code>dnsmasq.conf</code> configuration file by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo nano /etc/dnsmasq.conf"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"address=/api-int.<cluster_name>.<base_domain>/<IP_address>\naddress=/api-int.mycluster.example.com/192.168.1.10\naddress=/api-int.mycluster.example.com/2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Add a DNS PTR record to internally identify the API load balancer. For example, when using dnsmasq, modify the <code>dnsmasq.conf</code> configuration file by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo nano /etc/dnsmasq.conf"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"ptr-record=<IP_address>.in-addr.arpa,api-int.<cluster_name>.<base_domain>\nptr-record=10.1.168.192.in-addr.arpa,api-int.mycluster.example.com"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Restart the DNS server. For example, when using dnsmasq, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo systemctl restart dnsmasq"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>These records must be resolvable from all the nodes within the cluster.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-cleaning-up-previous-installations_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Cleaning up previous installations<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-cleaning-up-previous-installations_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You must remove the artifacts from any failed deployment attempt before trying to deploy OpenShift Container Platform again.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Power off all bare-metal nodes before installing the OpenShift Container Platform cluster by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ipmitool -I lanplus -U <user> -P <password> -H <management_server_ip> power off"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Remove all old bootstrap resources if any remain from an earlier deployment attempt by running the following script:
      <CodeBlock code={"for i in $(sudo virsh list | tail -n +3 | grep bootstrap | awk {'print $2'});\ndo\n  sudo virsh destroy $i;\n  sudo virsh undefine $i;\n  sudo virsh vol-delete $i --pool $i;\n  sudo virsh vol-delete $i.ign --pool $i;\n  sudo virsh pool-destroy $i;\n  sudo virsh pool-undefine $i;\ndone"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the artifacts that the earlier installation generated by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cd ; /bin/rm -rf auth/ bootstrap.ign master.ign worker.ign metadata.json \\\n.openshift_install.log .openshift_install_state.json"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Re-create the OpenShift Container Platform manifests by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ./openshift-baremetal-install --dir ~/clusterconfigs create manifests"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-registry-issues_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Issues with creating the registry<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-registry-issues_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">When creating a disconnected registry, you might encounter a "User Not Authorized" error when attempting to mirror the registry. This error might occur if you fail to append the new authentication to the existing <code>pull-secret.txt</code> file.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check to ensure authentication is successful by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ /usr/local/bin/oc adm release mirror \\\n  -a pull-secret-update.json\n  --from=$UPSTREAM_REPO \\\n  --to-release-image=$LOCAL_REG/$LOCAL_REPO:${VERSION} \\\n  --to=$LOCAL_REG/$LOCAL_REPO"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Example output of the variables used to mirror the install images:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"UPSTREAM_REPO=${RELEASE_IMAGE}\nLOCAL_REG=<registry_FQDN>:<registry_port>\nLOCAL_REPO='ocp4/openshift4'"} lang="terminal" />
          <p>The values of <code>RELEASE_IMAGE</code> and <code>VERSION</code> were set during the <em></em><em><strong>Retrieving OpenShift Installer</strong></em><em></em> step of the <em></em><em><strong>Setting up the environment for an OpenShift installation</strong></em><em></em> section.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      After mirroring the registry, confirm that you can access it in your disconnected environment by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ curl -k -u <user>:<password> https://registry.example.com:<registry_port>/v2/_catalog\n{\"repositories\":[\"<Repo_Name>\"]}"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-misc-issues_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Miscellaneous issues<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-misc-issues_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Review the miscellaneous OpenShift Container Platform issues to identify and resolve uncommon deployment errors in your cluster.</p>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-runtime-network-not-ready_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the <code>runtime network not ready</code> error<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-runtime-network-not-ready_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Troubleshoot the runtime network not ready error in your OpenShift Container Platform cluster to restore node communication.</p>
      <p>After the deployment of a cluster you might receive the following error:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"runtime network not ready: NetworkReady=false reason:NetworkPluginNotReady message:Network plugin returns error: Missing CNI default network"} lang="text" />
      <p>The Cluster Network Operator is responsible for deploying the networking components in response to a special object created by the installation program. It runs very early in the installation process, after the control plane nodes have come up, but before the bootstrap control plane has been torn down. It can be indicative of more subtle installation program issues, such as long delays in bringing up control plane nodes or issues with <code>apiserver</code> communication.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Inspect the pods in the <code>openshift-network-operator</code> namespace by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get all -n openshift-network-operator"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                    READY STATUS            RESTARTS   AGE\npod/network-operator-69dfd7b577-bg89v   0/1   ContainerCreating 0          149m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      On the <code>provisioner</code> node, determine that the network configuration exists by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ kubectl get network.config.openshift.io cluster -oyaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: config.openshift.io/v1\nkind: Network\nmetadata:\n  name: cluster\nspec:\n  serviceNetwork:\n  - 172.30.0.0/16\n  clusterNetwork:\n  - cidr: 10.128.0.0/14\n    hostPrefix: 23\n  networkType: OVNKubernetes"} lang="yaml" title="Example output" />
      <p>If it does not exist, the installation program did not create it. To determine why the installation program did not create it, run the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openshift-install create manifests"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check that the <code>network-operator</code> is running by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ kubectl -n openshift-network-operator get pods"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Retrieve the logs by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ kubectl -n openshift-network-operator logs -l \"name=network-operator\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>On high availability clusters with three or more control plane nodes, the Operator will perform leader election and all other Operators will sleep. For additional details, see <a href="https://github.com/openshift/installer/blob/master/docs/user/troubleshooting.md">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-no-matching-rootdevicehints_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the "No disk found with matching rootDeviceHints" error message<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-no-matching-rootdevicehints_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">To ensure the installer can locate and configure the target storage drive on your node, troubleshoot the No disk found with matching <code>rootDeviceHints</code> error. </p>
      <p>After you deploy a cluster, you might receive the following error message:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"No disk found with matching rootDeviceHints"} lang="text" />
      <p>To address the <code>No disk found with matching rootDeviceHints</code> error message, run the following temporary workaround:</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Change the <code>rootDeviceHints</code> to <code>minSizeGigabytes: 300</code>.</li>
      <li>
      After changing the <code>rootDeviceHints</code> settings, boot the CoreOS and then verify the disk information by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ udevadm info /dev/sda"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If you are using DL360 Gen 10 servers, be aware that they have an SD-card slot that might be assigned the <code>/dev/sda</code> device name. If no SD card is present in the server, it can cause conflicts. Ensure that the SD card slot is disabled in the server’s BIOS settings.</p>
      <p>If the <code>minSizeGigabytes</code> workaround is not fulfilling the requirements, you might need to revert <code>rootDeviceHints</code> back to <code>/dev/sda</code>. This change allows ironic images to boot successfully.</p>
      <p>An alternative approach to fixing this problem is by using the serial ID of the disk. However, be aware that finding the serial ID can be challenging and might make the configuration file less readable. If you select this path, ensure that you gather the serial ID using the previously documented command and incorporate it into your configuration.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-incorrect-ipv6-dhcp_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the cluster nodes not getting the correct IPv6 address over DHCP error<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-incorrect-ipv6-dhcp_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Troubleshoot and resolve DHCP configuration issues that prevent OpenShift Container Platform cluster nodes from receiving their assigned IPv6 addresses.</p>
      <p>If the cluster nodes are not getting the correct IPv6 address over DHCP, check the following procedure.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>Ensure the reserved IPv6 addresses reside outside the DHCP range.</li>
      <li>
      In the IP address reservation on the DHCP server, ensure the reservation specifies the correct DHCP Unique Identifier (DUID).
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"# This is a dnsmasq dhcp reservation, 'id:00:03:00:01' is the client id and '18:db:f2:8c:d5:9f' is the MAC Address for the NIC\nid:00:03:00:01:18:db:f2:8c:d5:9f,openshift-master-1,[2620:52:0:1302::6]"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Ensure that route announcements are working.</li>
      <li>Ensure that the DHCP server is listening on the required interfaces serving the IP address ranges.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-incorrect-hostname-dhcp_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the cluster nodes not getting the correct hostname over DHCP error<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-incorrect-hostname-dhcp_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">During IPv6 deployment, cluster nodes must get their hostname over DHCP. Sometimes the <code>NetworkManager</code> does not assign the hostname immediately. </p>
      <p>A control plane node might report an error such as:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Failed Units: 2\n  NetworkManager-wait-online.service\n  nodeip-configuration.service"} lang="text" />
      <p>This error indicates that the cluster node likely booted without first receiving a hostname from the DHCP server, which causes <code>kubelet</code> to boot with a <code>localhost.localdomain</code> hostname. To address the error, force the node to renew the hostname.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Retrieve the <code>hostname</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ hostname"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If the hostname is <code>localhost</code>, proceed with the following steps:</p>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Where <code>X</code> is the control plane node number.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Force the cluster node to renew the DHCP lease by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ sudo nmcli con up \"<bare_metal_nic>\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;bare_metal_nic&gt;</code> with the wired connection corresponding to the <code>baremetal</code> network.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>hostname</code> again by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ hostname"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      If the hostname is still <code>localhost.localdomain</code>, restart <code>NetworkManager</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>If the hostname is still <code>localhost.localdomain</code>, wait a few minutes and check again. If the hostname remains  <code>localhost.localdomain</code>, repeat the previous steps.</li>
      <li>
      Restart the <code>nodeip-configuration</code> service by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ sudo systemctl restart nodeip-configuration.service"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>This service will reconfigure the <code>kubelet</code> service with the correct hostname references.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Reload the unit files definition since the kubelet changed in the previous step by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ sudo systemctl daemon-reload"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Restart the <code>kubelet</code> service by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ sudo systemctl restart kubelet.service"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Ensure <code>kubelet</code> booted with the correct hostname by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master-X ~]$ sudo journalctl -fu kubelet.service"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If the cluster node is not getting the correct hostname over DHCP after the cluster is up and running, such as during a reboot, the cluster will have a pending <code>csr</code>. <em></em><em><strong>Do not</strong></em><em></em> approve a <code>csr</code>, or other issues might arise.</p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      <em></em><em><strong>Addressing a </strong></em><code>csr</code><em></em>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get CSRs on the cluster by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csr"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify if a pending <code>csr</code> contains <code>Subject Name: localhost.localdomain</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csr <pending_csr> -o jsonpath='{.spec.request}' | base64 --decode | openssl req -noout -text"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Remove any <code>csr</code> that contains <code>Subject Name: localhost.localdomain</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete csr <wrong_csr>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-unreachable-routes_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the routes not reaching endpoints error<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-unreachable-routes_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">To restore external network connectivity to your applications, troubleshoot OpenShift Container Platform routes that do not reach their service endpoints. </p>
      <p>During the installation process, it is possible to encounter a Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) conflict.</p>
      <p>This conflict occurs when a currently active OpenShift Container Platform node from an older deployment uses your current OpenShift Container Platform cluster name without actually being part of the new deployment.</p>
      <p>For example, a cluster was deployed using the cluster name <code>openshift</code>, deploying three control plane nodes and three worker nodes. Later, a separate install uses the same cluster name <code>openshift</code>, but this redeployment only installed three control plane nodes, leaving the three worker nodes from a previous deployment in an <code>ON</code> state. This might cause a Virtual Router Identifier (VRID) conflict and a VRRP conflict.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the route by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get route oauth-openshift"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check the service endpoint by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get svc oauth-openshift"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME              TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)   AGE\noauth-openshift   ClusterIP   172.30.19.162   <none>        443/TCP   59m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Attempt to reach the service from a control plane node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@master0 ~]$ curl -k https://172.30.19.162"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"{\n  \"kind\": \"Status\",\n  \"apiVersion\": \"v1\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n  },\n  \"status\": \"Failure\",\n  \"message\": \"forbidden: User \\\"system:anonymous\\\" cannot get path \\\"/\\\"\",\n  \"reason\": \"Forbidden\",\n  \"details\": {\n  },\n  \"code\": 403\n}"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Identify the <code>authentication-operator</code> errors from the <code>provisioner</code> node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc logs deployment/authentication-operator -n openshift-authentication-operator"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"Event(v1.ObjectReference{Kind:\"Deployment\", Namespace:\"openshift-authentication-operator\", Name:\"authentication-operator\", UID:\"225c5bd5-b368-439b-9155-5fd3c0459d98\", APIVersion:\"apps/v1\", ResourceVersion:\"\", FieldPath:\"\"}): type: 'Normal' reason: 'OperatorStatusChanged' Status for clusteroperator/authentication changed: Degraded message changed from \"IngressStateEndpointsDegraded: All 2 endpoints for oauth-server are reporting\""} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>Ensure that the cluster name for every deployment is unique, ensuring no conflict.</li>
      <li>Turn off all the rogue nodes which are not part of the cluster deployment that are using the same cluster name. Otherwise, the authentication pod of the  OpenShift Container Platform cluster might never start successfully.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-failed-ignition-during-firstboot_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the failed Ignition during firstboot error<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-failed-ignition-during-firstboot_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Troubleshoot and resolve Ignition configuration failures that occur during the firstboot of your nodes to prevent cluster deployment failures.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Connect to the node where the Ignition configuration failed:
      <CodeBlock code={"Failed Units: 1\n  machine-config-daemon-firstboot.service"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Restart the <code>machine-config-daemon-firstboot</code> service by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"[core@worker-X ~]$ sudo systemctl restart machine-config-daemon-firstboot.service"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-ntp-out-of-sync_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Addressing the NTP out of sync error<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-ntp-out-of-sync_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">The deployment of OpenShift Container Platform clusters depends on NTP synchronized clocks among the cluster nodes. Without synchronized clocks, the deployment may fail due to clock drift if the time difference is greater than two seconds.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Check for differences in the <code>AGE</code> of the cluster nodes by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                         STATUS   ROLES    AGE   VERSION\nmaster-0.cloud.example.com   Ready    master   145m   v1.35.4\nmaster-1.cloud.example.com   Ready    master   135m   v1.35.4\nmaster-2.cloud.example.com   Ready    master   145m   v1.35.4\nworker-2.cloud.example.com   Ready    worker   100m   v1.35.4"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check for inconsistent timing delays due to clock drift by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get bmh -n openshift-machine-api"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"master-1   error registering master-1  ipmi://<out_of_band_ip>"} lang="terminal" title="Example output showing registration error due to clock drift" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Check <code>System clock synchronized</code> on a node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo timedatectl"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"               Local time: Tue 2020-03-10 18:20:02 UTC\n           Universal time: Tue 2020-03-10 18:20:02 UTC\n                 RTC time: Tue 2020-03-10 18:36:53\n                Time zone: UTC (UTC, +0000)\nSystem clock synchronized: no\n              NTP service: active\n          RTC in local TZ: no"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>Ensure that the <code>System clock synchronized</code> value is <em></em><em><strong>yes</strong></em><em></em> before proceeding with the installation.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Resolve clock drift based on your deployment stage:
      <ul>
      <li>
      <em></em><em><strong>Addressing clock synchronization for clock drift in an existing cluster:</strong></em><em></em>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a Butane config file including the contents of the <code>chrony.conf</code> file to be delivered to the nodes. In the following example, create <code>99-master-chrony.bu</code> to add the file to the control plane nodes. You can modify the file for compute nodes or repeat this procedure for the compute role.
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>See "Creating machine configs with Butane" for information about Butane.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <CodeBlock code={"variant: openshift\nversion: {{ product_version }}.0\nmetadata:\n  name: 99-master-chrony\n  labels:\n    machineconfiguration.openshift.io/role: master\nstorage:\n  files:\n  - path: /etc/chrony.conf\n    mode: 0644\n    overwrite: true\n    contents:\n      inline: |\n        server <NTP_server> iburst\n        stratumweight 0\n        driftfile /var/lib/chrony/drift\n        rtcsync\n        makestep 10 3\n        bindcmdaddress 127.0.0.1\n        bindcmdaddress ::1\n        keyfile /etc/chrony.keys\n        commandkey 1\n        generatecommandkey\n        noclientlog\n        logchange 0.5\n        logdir /var/log/chrony"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;NTP_server&gt;</code> with the IP address of the NTP server.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Use Butane to generate a <code>MachineConfig</code> object file, <code>99-master-chrony.yaml</code>, containing the configuration to be delivered to the nodes by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ butane 99-master-chrony.bu -o 99-master-chrony.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>MachineConfig</code> object file by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f 99-master-chrony.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Ensure the <code>System clock synchronized</code> value is <em></em><em><strong>yes</strong></em><em></em> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sudo timedatectl"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"               Local time: Tue 2020-03-10 19:10:02 UTC\n           Universal time: Tue 2020-03-10 19:10:02 UTC\n                 RTC time: Tue 2020-03-10 19:36:53\n                Time zone: UTC (UTC, +0000)\nSystem clock synchronized: yes\n              NTP service: active\n          RTC in local TZ: no"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      <em></em><em><strong>Addressing clock synchronization for clock drift before deployment:</strong></em><em></em>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Generate the manifest files and add this file to the <code>openshift</code> directory.
      <p>For example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cp chrony-masters.yaml ~/clusterconfigs/openshift/99_masters-chrony-configuration.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Continue to create the cluster.</li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="ipi-install-troubleshooting-reviewing-the-installation_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="group scroll-mt-20">Reviewing the installation<a href="#ipi-install-troubleshooting-reviewing-the-installation_ipi-install-troubleshooting" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">After installation, ensure the installation program deployed the nodes and pods successfully.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      When the OpenShift Container Platform cluster nodes are installed appropriately, ensure the following <code>Ready</code> state within the <code>STATUS</code> column by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                   STATUS   ROLES           AGE  VERSION\nmaster-0.example.com   Ready    master,worker   4h   v1.35.4\nmaster-1.example.com   Ready    master,worker   4h   v1.35.4\nmaster-2.example.com   Ready    master,worker   4h   v1.35.4"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Confirm the installation program deployed all pods successfully. Remove any pods that are still running or have completed as part of the output by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pods --all-namespaces | grep -iv running | grep -iv complete"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
    </article>
